ced early retirement at age 60 for workers with 30 years of service; disability annuities for those totally or occupationally disabled; and spousal and survivor annuities. Survivor benefits are often more generous than Social Security equivalents because they include Tier II components. Divorced spouses and dependent children may also qualify for specific portions of these benefits.\n\nSeparately, the RUIA provides daily unemployment and sickness benefits financed exclusively by employer payroll taxes, which vary based on claim history. Eligibility requires specific earnings and service thresholds in the prior year. Benefits are capped at a maximum daily rate (reduced by sequestration to approximately $72.23 as of 2018) and are payable for up to 26 weeks, with an additional 13 weeks of extended benefits available for workers with at least 10 years of service. The RRB also provides free job placement services to those claiming unemployment benefits. Overall, the RRB system operates as a hybrid of Social Security and private pension structures, tailored to the unique employment history and needs of the railroad industry.', refusal=None, role='assistant', annotations=None, audio=None, function_call=None, tool_calls=[], reasoning=None), stop_reason=None, token_ids=None)], created=1783799480, model='Qwen3.5-27B', object='chat.completion', service_tier=None, system_fingerprint=None, usage=CompletionUsage(completion_tokens=627, prompt_tokens=4926, total_tokens=5553, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), prompt_logprobs=None, prompt_token_ids=None, kv_transfer_params=None)

