m, and military awards. Following mass shootings at Fort Hood (2009), Little Rock (2009), and Chattanooga (2015), Congress passed the National Defense Authorization Act (NDAA) for Fiscal Year 2015. This legislation redefined "foreign terrorist organization" attacks to include incidents where the perpetrator was inspired or motivated by such groups, even if the act occurred on U.S. soil. Consequently, victims of the Fort Hood and Little Rock shootings were retroactively awarded Purple Hearts. This precedent sparked further debate regarding the Oklahoma City bombing and the Orlando nightclub shooting, raising questions about the subjectivity of eligibility and whether the award should be granted for criminal acts versus acts of war.\n\nLegislative efforts have also focused on preserving the integrity of the medal. Recent bills, such as the "Private Corrado Piccoli Purple Heart Preservation Act," have sought to criminalize the sale of Purple Hearts to prevent them from becoming commercial memorabilia. Additionally, the NDAA for Fiscal Year 2019 extended certain morale, welfare, and recreation privileges to Purple Heart recipients and their caregivers. As the military continues to face evolving threats and new types of injuries, the report concludes that Congress and the executive branch will likely continue to grapple with balancing the need to honor all forms of service-related sacrifice against the desire to maintain the specific combat-focused legacy of the Purple Heart.', refusal=None, role='assistant', annotations=None, audio=None, function_call=None, tool_calls=[], reasoning=None), stop_reason=None, token_ids=None)], created=1783799710, model='Qwen3.5-27B', object='chat.completion', service_tier=None, system_fingerprint=None, usage=CompletionUsage(completion_tokens=608, prompt_tokens=6571, total_tokens=7179, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), prompt_logprobs=None, prompt_token_ids=None, kv_transfer_params=None)

